  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g3820800",
    "@type": [
      "https://schema.org/BlogPosting"
    ],
    "https://schema.org/text": [
      {
        "@language": "fr",
        "@value": "Understanding the Options for IRSSA Records\n"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g3820840",
    "@type": [
      "https://schema.org/BlogPosting"
    ],
    "https://schema.org/text": [
      {
        "@language": "fr",
        "@value": "If this feels emotionally heavy or brings up difficult memories, support is available. Visit downiewenjack.ca/mental-health-resources.\nWhat are IAP and ADR Records\nThe Independent Assessment Process (IAP) and earlier Alternative Dispute Resolution (ADR) were compensation processes established under the Indian Residential Schools Settlement Agreement (IRSSA) for Survivors who experienced abuse in residential schools in Canada.\nThrough the IAP and ADR processes, Survivors were required to share deeply personal information and testimony as part of their claims. These records include:\n\nApplicant’s name and personal information\nWritten and audio testimony from hearings\nDecisions made by adjudicators\nCompensation awarded\n\nThe records describe the abuse suffered and how it affected the Survivor’s life. They may include information about health, employment, criminal history, and other sensitive information.\nUnder a Supreme Court decision, IAP and ADR records are scheduled to be destroyed on September 19, 2027, unless a Survivor chooses otherwise. This timeline is part of a legal ruling to protect privacy and confidentiality.\nFor individuals who were part of a claim, or for families supporting a loved one, there is still time to decide what will happen to the records.\nWhat are Your Options?\nSurvivors have four options regarding their IAP or ADR records:\n\nDo Nothing. Records remain confidential and will be destroyed in 2027.\nRequest a personal copy. Records can be kept privately or shared at the Survivor’s discretion.\nPreserve them at the National Centre for Truth and Reconciliation (NCTR). They may be used for history, public education, and research.\nRequest a copy and preserve the records at the NCTR.\n\nThe choice is deeply personal and belongs solely to Survivors and their loved ones.\nPreserving Your Records\nIf a Survivor chooses to preserve their records at the NCTR, they may decide how those records are made available.\n\nRestricted Access: The NCTR may use and share the records for education and research after removing personally identifying information.\nOpen Access: The NCTR may use and share the records, including the Survivor’s name, for education and research purposes. Certain personal details, such as address and contact information, will still be removed.\n\nIn both cases, all information that identifies other people will be removed before records are transferred to the NCTR. The NCTR is committed to the respectful and dignified use of these records and of doing no harm.\nWhy Preservation Matters\nBy deciding what happens to their records, Survivors and their families continue to have autonomy over their lived experiences and deeply personal stories. Choosing to preserve their records ensures that individual and collective voices and those silenced by history for far too long remain part of the ongoing work of understanding, healing, and reconciliation. These records can become powerful teaching tools for future generations and contribute to broader public awareness throughout Canada and beyond, but this decision must come from the Survivors and their families.\nPetition to Save Files\nA petition calling on Parliament to safeguard all residential school records held by the government, churches, and institutions has received more than 13,000 signatures. Petition organizers and supporters argue that destroying the files risks the “erasure of Survivor voices, loss of historical memory, and obstacles to legal research, accountability, and restorative processes.” Conservative MP Billy Morin has supported the petition. The petition closed on March 5.\nFor additional information or to download the request and consent forms, visit the NCTR website or contact the IAP Secretariat: https://nctr.ca/records/preserve-your-records/\nSources :\nhttps://nctr.ca/records/preserve-your-records/\nhttps://www.ourcommons.ca/petitions/en/Petition/Details?Petition=e-6909\nhttps://www.cbc.ca/news/indigenous/petition-residential-school-files-9.7114976\nhttps://www.cbc.ca/news/politics/indian-residential-schools-records-supreme-court-1.4343259\n"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
