  {
    "@id": "_:g3922740",
    "@type": [
      "https://schema.org/ImageObject"
    ],
    "https://schema.org/thumbnailUrl": [
      {
        "@id": "https://downiewenjack.ca/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/bill5.jpg"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g3922780",
    "@type": [
      "https://schema.org/BlogPosting"
    ],
    "https://schema.org/text": [
      {
        "@language": "fr",
        "@value": "What is Bill 5 (Ontario)?\nLe projet de loi n° 5, officiellement intitulé « Loi de 2025 visant à protéger l'Ontario en dynamisant notre économie », a été adopté par le gouvernement de l'Ontario le 5 juin 2025. Ce texte apporte des modifications importantes à dix lois provinciales, notamment l'abrogation de la Loi de 2007 sur les espèces en voie de disparition et de la Loi sur la Société Ontario Place, ainsi que la création d'une nouvelle Loi sur les zones économiques spéciales.\nAccording to the Ontario government, these changes aim to reduce regulatory delays and increase investment in large-scale economic development projects. First Nations communities, legal scholars, and environmental advocates have expressed concern about the bill’s implications for Indigenous rights, environmental protections, and public participation in decision-making.\nKey Changes Introduced by Bill 5\nSpecial Economic Zones\nThe Special Economic Zones Act gives the Cabinet the authority to designate specific areas for accelerated development. In these zones, provincial laws and municipal bylaws can be set aside. The Cabinet may also appoint trusted proponents to carry out these projects. Several First Nations have raised concerns that this structure could lead to development on their territories without proper consultation or consent.\nRepeal of the Endangered Species Act\nThe Endangered Species Act, 2007 has been repealed and replaced by the Species Conservation Act, 2025. The new act narrows the definition of habitat, introduces more steps before a species can be protected, and removes automatic protections. The government argues this will reduce duplication and streamline approvals, but environmental experts and Indigenous communities say it weakens important safeguards for land and wildlife.\nMining and Resource Development\nChanges to the Mining Act are intended to shorten project approval timelines and streamline permitting. These reforms are expected to support Ontario’s critical minerals strategy, particularly in the Ring of Fire. However, many First Nations in Treaty 9 territory have not provided Free, Prior, and Informed Consent. Some communities are preparing legal challenges to protect their rights and lands.\nInfrastructure and Land Use Planning\nBill 5 amends the Environmental Assessment Act, the Planning Act, and several others to speed up infrastructure and energy projects. The repeal of the Ontario Place Corporation Act also allows for greater provincial control over development plans. These changes reduce oversight and public involvement in land use decisions.\nIndigenous and Community Concerns\nIndigenous Rights and FPIC\nMany First Nations have stated that Bill 5 was introduced and passed without meaningful consultation. The legislation permits major developments to move forward without community consent or access to legal recourse. A core element of the United Nations Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples (UNDRIP) is Free, Prior, and Informed Consent (FPIC). FPIC requires that Indigenous Peoples be informed about developments that affect them, be given time to consider impacts, and have the ability to consent or decline without coercion.\nWithout FPIC, legislation like Bill 5 undermines Indigenous self-determination and Treaty relationships. Indigenous leaders have emphasized that consent is not optional and must be at the centre of any decision involving their lands.\nEnvironmental Protection\nBy weakening environmental laws and removing key protections, Bill 5 limits the ability to prevent degradation of lands and ecosystems. This disproportionately affects Indigenous Nations and communities that rely on healthy environments for cultural, economic, and spiritual practices.\nTransparency and Accountability\nThe bill consolidates power within the Cabinet and restricts legal and community oversight. Many groups argue that this shift in governance makes it more difficult for affected communities to hold the province or developers accountable.\nHow This Relates to the Federal Bill C-5\nThe goals of Ontario’s Bill 5 reflect those found in Canada’s federal Bill C-5, also introduced in 2025. Indigenous communities call both Bill 5 and Bill C-5 a clear and present danger to First Nations’ self-determination rights.\nBill C-5, known as the One Canadian Economy Act, includes measures to speed up approval for large-scale infrastructure projects by reducing federal regulatory requirements.\nThe Building Canada Act, one part of Bill C-5, allows federal ministers to approve projects deemed to be in the national interest. This approval can override multiple federal laws and reduce the need for separate environmental or permitting reviews. Critics have warned that this framework limits public consultation and does not uphold the standard of FPIC.\nLegal and political responses from Indigenous Nations and communities have been strong. The Assembly of First Nations, the Chiefs of Ontario, and numerous other organizations have stated that Bill C-5 does not meet the requirements of constitutional consultation and violates the principles of UNDRIP. Several First Nations have stated that they were not meaningfully consulted and have raised concerns that the bill will limit their ability to protect their lands and communities.\nEnvironmental organizations have also raised alarms, stating that Bill C-5 allows projects to proceed without adequate environmental review. Concerns have been raised about the concentration of decision-making power within the federal Cabinet and the potential impacts on both ecosystems and Indigenous rights.\nMost recently, nine First Nations in Ontario are asking the court for an injunction against Bill 5 and Bill C-5, which would prohibit some of the most contentious aspects, including the implementation of the “Special Economic Zones” in Ontario.\nBill C-61\nThe passage of both Bill C-5 and Bill 5 has also drawn attention to Bill C-61. Bill C-61, the First Nations Clean Water Act, is a federal bill introduced in 2023. It recognizes the right of First Nations to self-government in relation to water and affirms that access to clean drinking water is a human right.\nThe bill aims to ensure that First Nations have access to safe drinking water, source water protection, wastewater services, and the related infrastructure located on, in, or under First Nations lands. It proposes a rights-based regulatory pathway that supports First Nations in creating and enforcing their own water protection laws, grounded in Indigenous legal traditions, knowledge systems, and community needs.\nIn the bill, the federal government has also committed to making best efforts to provide adequate and sustainable funding for water services on First Nations lands that are comparable to those received in non-First Nations communities.\nRecent controversy emerged when the Environment Ministers of Ontario and Alberta sent a letter to the federal government asking them to refrain from introducing legislation that would require Canada to provide clean drinking water to First Nations communities. Following public backlash and political confusion, Ontario’s Minister of the Environment, Todd McCarthy, apologized. Indigenous leaders have characterized this as another example of governments speaking on their behalf without consent.\nSeveral First Nations, including the Nishnawbe Aski Nation (NAN), have strongly opposed Ontario and Alberta’s involvement in Bill C-61. NAN leadership has stated that Ontario and Alberta have “no business” participating in legislation meant to uphold Treaty rights and fulfill federal obligations to First Nations. As emphasized by Grand Chief Alvin Fiddler, clean drinking water is a Treaty right and should not be subject to provincial control or conditions.\nFirst Nations have raised concerns that Ontario’s involvement in Bill C-61, especially in the context of Bill 5’s regulatory rollbacks, could create additional barriers to self-determination.\nWhy This Matters\nTogether, Ontario’s Bill 5, Canada’s Bill C-5, and the contested role of Ontario and Alberta in Bill C-61 reflect a broader legislative trend aimed at accelerating development projects by removing regulatory barriers. While governments argue that these changes are necessary for economic growth, they have been passed in ways that many Indigenous communities and advocacy groups see as dismissive of their rights and voices.\nEconomic development that does not respect Indigenous governance, environmental stewardship, or legal obligations risks deepening existing harms. First Nations leaders have emphasized that progress cannot come at the cost of consent, land, and culture. The full and meaningful involvement of Indigenous Peoples is essential for any development to be truly sustainable and just.\nRecent Developments and Ongoing Court Challenges\nOpposition to Ontario’s Bill 5 and Canada’s Bill C-5 has continued to grow since both laws were passed. As noted above, nine First Nations asked the court for an injunction in Ontario and have since filed a constitutional challenge in the Ontario Superior Court of Justice, arguing that the legislation violates Indigenous rights, undermines Treaty relationships, and weakens environmental protections by allowing major projects to proceed without meaningful consultation or consent.\nAn injunction is a court order that directs a person or organization to act or refrain from acting and is enforceable by law. These injunctions seek to pause some of the most contentious elements of the legislation, including the implementation of “Special Economic Zones” under Bill 5 and the expanded federal approval powers under Bill C-5. These provisions, they argue, concentrate decision-making authority within the Cabinet while limiting the ability of Indigenous Nations and the public to participate in or challenge development decisions that affect their lands.\nMore recently, an additional five First Nations have joined the legal challenge, including Walpole Island First Nation, Kashechewan First Nation, Mississaugas of the Credit First Nation, Wapekeka First Nation, and Wunnumin Lake First Nation. Together, these Nations have raised serious concerns that Bill 5 and Bill C-5 grant the governments sweeping authority to override existing laws and proceed with development without upholding the constitutional duty to consult or the standard of\nFPIC.\nThese legal actions address a broader concern shared by Indigenous leaders throughout the country: that fast-tracking economic development without respecting Indigenous governance, Treaty rights, and environmental stewardship risks repeating patterns of colonialism rather than advancing reconciliation. The outcome of these cases will have significant implications for Indigenous self-determination, environmental protection, and how governments interpret their legal obligations moving forward.\nSources \nBill C-5 reveals fault lines between Ottawa and Indigenous peoples over consultation, consent – The Globe and Mail \nNational Chief appeals to senators to slow approval of Bill C-5 \nOntario’s controversial Bill 5, explained | The Narwhal \nDoug Ford’s Bill 5 is now law in Ontario. Here’s what happens next | CBC News \nAlberta, Ontario don’t want feds to reintroduce First Nations safe drinking water law \nOntario environment minister apologizes for ‘confusion’ over clean water bill letter | CBC News \nBill C-61: First Nations Clean Water Act (short title), or an Act respecting water, source water, drinking water, wastewater and related infrastructure on First Nation lands – Canada.ca \n9 Ontario First Nations ask for injunction against Bill 5, say law represents ‘clear and present danger’ | CBC News \n5 more First Nations join legal challenge against Bill 5 and C-5 | CBC News \nFirst Nations in Ontario challenge provincial, federal project laws \nWhat Does an Injunction Do and How Does It Work? – LegalClarity \nUNDRIP_Handbook_WEB.pdf \n"
      }
    ]
  },
