  {
    "@id": "_:g4835520",
    "@type": [
      "https://schema.org/BlogPosting"
    ],
    "https://schema.org/text": [
      {
        "@language": "fr",
        "@value": "Holiday Gift Guide: Shop Indigenous Businesses\n"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g4835560",
    "@type": [
      "https://schema.org/ImageObject"
    ],
    "https://schema.org/thumbnailUrl": [
      {
        "@id": "https://downiewenjack.ca/wp-content/uploads/2023/11/giftguide.jpg"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g4835600",
    "@type": [
      "https://schema.org/BlogPosting"
    ],
    "https://schema.org/text": [
      {
        "@language": "fr",
        "@value": "To help you take #reconciliACTION while you shop for your loved ones this holiday season, we’ve put together DWF’s first-ever Holiday Gift Guide. Below you will find 12 Indigenous-owned businesses, the new DWF Merch Store, and of course, The Bay’s iconic Point Blankets, which support Oshki Wupoowane | The Blanket Fund. Happy Holidays!\n\nClothing & Apparel\n\nDWF’s NEW Merch Store!\nAll proceeds from the Merch Store go to the Gord Downie & Chanie Wenjack Fund and support our work to build cultural understanding and create a path toward reconciliation between Indigenous and non-Indigenous peoples. Check out our t-shirts, hats, and more!\n\nGord Downie & Chanie Wenjack Fund logo Zip Hoodie\nUnisex fit grey zip-up hoodie with DWF logo on the front.\nTo ensure your order arrives in time for the holidays, order before December 11 if you’re in Ontario, or before November 27 if you’re outside Ontario.\n\n\n\nBROWSE DWF’S MERCH STORE\n\n\n\n\nAniin Retail Inc.\nFrom beautifully crafted jewelry to traditional clothing and cozy knitwear, Aniin carries collections from well-known Indigenous artists, designers, and makers.\n\nAaniin Original Beanie\nThe Aaniin Original Beanie features syllabics that say ‘hello’ in Anishinabemowin.\n\n\n\nBROWSE ANIIN RETAIL INC.\n\n\n\n\nMINI TIPI\nMINI TIPI collaborates with Indigenous artists to design textiles to create high-quality products. They manufacture their products locally in Gatineau, QC.\n\nConnections Reversible Blanket – Emily Kewageshig X MINI TIPI\nDWF Artist Ambassador, Emily Kewageshig, collaborated with MINI TIPI to design the Connections Reversible Blanket. It features the hummingbird, which “symbolizes healing and the connection that we have to the beyond.”\n\n\n\nBROWSE MINI TIPI\n\n\n\n\nMetis Branded\nEstablished by a young Métis entrepreneur and mother of two, Metis Branded is a small business located in Winnipeg, MB. They love to take vintage items and repurpose them, keeping them out of landfills.\n\nMétis Sash Ribbon Skirt\nMade to order, their Métis Sash Ribbon Skirt is customized to your size and colour preferences.\n\n\n\nBROWSE METIS BRANDED\n\n\n\n\nArt\n\nPatrick Hunter Art & Design\nPatrick Hunter is a Two-Spirit, Ojibwe painter, graphic designer and award-winning entrepreneur from Red Lake, ON. He paints what he sees through a spiritual lens—inspired by his homeland, surrounded by the original works of Woodland painter Norval Morrisseau throughout his community.\n\n“Lakeside Dreams for Two”\n\n\n\nBROWSE PATRICK HUNTER ART & DESIGN\n\n\n\n\nCJ Houle Fine Art\nCody James Houle, the creative force behind this business, is a talented Indigenous artist whose work reflects a deep connection to Indigenous culture and nature.\n\n“7 Grandfather Teachings”\n\n\n\nBROWSE CJ HOULE FINE ART\n\n\n\n\nGiftware, Home Décor & More!\n\nThe Bay’s Point Blankets\n\nThe Bay’s Point Blankets\nNet proceeds from the sale of The Bay’s Point Blankets go toward Oshki Wupoowane | The Blanket Fund which supports Indigenous cultural, artistic, and educational activities. Check out some of the projects that have been funded so far through The Blanket Fund.\n\n\n\nSHOP THE BAY’S POINT BLANKETS\n\n\n\n\nGoodminds First Nations, Métis, and Inuit Books\nGoodminds is a leading source for Indigenous literature, specializing in First Nations, Inuit, and Métis books.\n\nMoon of the Turning Leaves by Waubgeshig Rice\nJust released this fall, this novel by Artist Ambassador Waubgeshig Rice is “an epic journey to a forgotten homeland. The hotly anticipated sequel to the bestselling novel Moon of the Crusted Snow.”\n\n\n\nBROWSE GOODMINDS\n\n\n\n\nNative Love Notes\nNative Love Notes is “a little rezzy, a little deadly, and 100% Indigenous owned.” It’s run by Amy from Opaskwayak Cree Nation and is located on Treaty One Territory (Winnipeg).\n\nUr Sacrit Poster\nNative Love Notes creates museum-quality posters like this one, made on thick and durable matte paper.\n\n\n\nBROWSE NATIVE LOVE NOTES\n\n\n\n\nIndigenous Box\n“Indigenous Box’s mission is to promote and elevate the good work being done by Indigenous entrepreneurs.” Not only do they sell boxes that feature items from Indigenous businesses, but they also have a marketplace where you can shop for specific items directly.\n\n“MMIW Awareness” Box\nThis box features items that will help you raise awareness for missing and murdered Indigenous women, girls, transgender, gender-diverse, and Two-Spirit people who continue to experience violence at a disproportionate rate.\n\n\n\nBROWSE INDIGENOUS BOX\n\n\n\n\nFood\n\nBangin’ Bannock\nFrom their signature bannock loaves to their exciting twists like bannock pizza bases and bannock donuts, their offerings are a delightful journey that takes you to the heart of Indigenous culinary heritage.\n\nOG Mix\n“400g of Original Bangin’ Bannock dry mix! Fried version instructions included on bag, makes approx. 8 pieces of Frybread.”\n\n\n\nBROWSE BANGIN’ BANNOCK\n\n\n\n\nWabanaki Maple\nLocated on Neqotkuk (Tobique) First Nation in New Brunswick, they produce organic, high-quality maple syrup using sustainable practices. Their product line goes beyond the classic maple syrup, offering bourbon, whiskey, and toasted oak-infused syrups, perfect for adding an exciting twist to your holiday dishes or gifting a gourmet treat.\n\nMini Maple Bundle Set by Wabanaki\n“Elevate your culinary experiences with our elegantly packaged trio of 100ml bottles, each brimming with pure maple syrup aged with the rich essence of Bourbon, Whiskey, and Toasted Oak barrels.”\n\n\n\nBROWSE WABANAKI MAPLE\n\n\n\n\nBeauty Products\n\nUasau Soaps\n“Uasau (pronounced ooh-ah-sow) draws its inspiration from prehistoric Inuit tradition and the Nuna. They create sustainably sourced items made with Traditional Inuit Practices. The entire community comes together to cooperate in a life-sustaining effort passed down for countless generations. Inuit are known for their resourcefulness and not wasting any part of an animal. Uasau follows suit, only using bowhead whale oil from community hunts.”\n\nLemon Bowhead Body Butter by UASAU\nThis body butter also has bowhead whale oil and grape seed carrier oil. Moisturize, luxuriate and heal your skin with this lemon-scented body butter.\n\n\n\nBROWSE UASAU\n\n\n\n\nCheekbone Beauty\n“Cheekbone’s aim is to make a difference in the lives of Indigenous youth through donations that support educational opportunities for them, and to create a space in the beauty industry where everyone, including Indigenous people, feels represented and seen.”\n\nJenn’s Faves\n“Curated by Founder and CEO Jenn Harper, this bundle contains her favourite Cheekbone Beauty products that she uses for an everyday full-face look.”\n\n\n\nBROWSE CHEEKBONE BEAUTY\n\n\n\n"
      }
    ]
  },
