    ],
    "https://schema.org/text": [
      {
        "@language": "fr",
        "@value": "Politique de confidentialité Règles de la communauté\n"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://downiewenjack.ca/temporary/a241e7d7-eff8-4e75-8d39-3f95d25a0f4f",
    "@type": [
      "https://schema.org/CreativeWork"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasDerivedFrom": [
      {
        "@id": "https://downiewenjack.ca/fr/pressrelease-wr/"
      }
    ],
    "https://schema.org/headline": [
      {
        "@language": "fr",
        "@value": "Politique en matière d'IA chez DWF        "
      }
    ],
    "https://schema.org/text": [
      {
        "@language": "fr",
        "@value": "Gord Downie & Chanie Wenjack Fund des technologies d'intelligence artificielle sécurisées et spécialement conçues pour améliorer la manière dont nous fournissons des ressources et soutenons notre réseau. Ces outils permettent de maintenir les informations à jour à l'aide de données accessibles au public, ce qui réduit les délais, minimise le gaspillage et garantit que les ressources parviennent bien à leur destinataire. Le respect de la vie privée est primordial ; aucune information personnelle n'est partagée en dehors du DWF, et vous pouvez vous désinscrire à tout moment.\n            "
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://downiewenjack.ca/temporary/908b19bf-3fd0-40b9-86a5-10a61ae59d74",
    "@type": [
      "https://schema.org/CreativeWork"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasDerivedFrom": [
      {
        "@id": "https://downiewenjack.ca/fr/pressrelease-wr/"
      }
    ],
    "https://schema.org/text": [
      {
        "@language": "fr",
        "@value": "À propos de DWF\nInspiré par l’histoire de Chanie et par l’appel de Gord à bâtir un Canada meilleur, le Gord Downie & Chanie Wenjack Fund à favoriser la compréhension culturelle et Gord Downie & Chanie Wenjack Fund ouvrir la voie à la réconciliation entre les peuples autochtone et non autochtones. Notre objectif est d’améliorer la vie des autochtone en sensibilisant la population, en favorisant l’éducation et en tissant des liens entre tous les peuples du Canada.\nAbonnez-vous à notre newsletter\n"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://downiewenjack.ca/temporary/26a0ec74-8145-404b-b9ec-0951ff258767",
    "@type": [
      "https://schema.org/CreativeWork"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasDerivedFrom": [
      {
        "@id": "https://downiewenjack.ca/fr/pressrelease-wr/"
      }
    ],
    "https://schema.org/text": [
      {
        "@language": "fr",
        "@value": "Contactez-nous\nE-mail : staff@downiewenjack.ca\nNuméro gratuit : 1-833-666-2414 \nNuméro local : 519-964-0393 \nAdresse :\nBoîte postale 749\nOHSWEKEN, ON\nN0A1M0\n"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g5827260",
    "@type": [
      "https://schema.org/ImageObject"
    ],
    "https://schema.org/thumbnailUrl": [
      {
        "@id": "https://downiewenjack.ca/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/Recommended-reading-2025-1.jpg"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g5827300",
    "@type": [
      "https://schema.org/BlogPosting"
    ],
    "https://schema.org/text": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Recommended Reading in Honour of the National Day for Truth and Reconciliation 2025\n"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g5827340",
    "@type": [
      "https://schema.org/BlogPosting"
    ],
    "https://schema.org/text": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The National Day for Truth and Reconciliation and Orange Shirt Day are crucial moments for reflection and learning about the true history of Canada and the ongoing impacts of residential schools and other colonial systems. To support you on your journey toward reconciliation, DWF has compiled a list of recommended readings to deepen your understanding. Each book offers a unique perspective and insight into Indigenous cultures and experiences.\n\n\n52 Ways to Reconcile: How to Walk with Indigenous Peoples on the Path to Healing\nAuthor: David A. Robertson\nDescription: From bestselling author of the Misewa Saga series David A. Robertson, this practical guide offers 52 small, actionable steps—one for each week of the year—to help readers engage in meaningful reconciliation with Indigenous communities.\n\n\nDecolonization and Me: Conversations about Healing a Nation and Ourselves\nAuthor: Phyllis Webstad & Kristy McLeod\nDescription: This book challenges readers through a series of sensitive conversations exploring decolonization, Indigenization, healing, and every person’s individual responsibility to truth and reconciliation. Centred around the Orange Shirt Day movement and the National Day for Truth and Reconciliation, these conversations encourage readers to unpack and reckon with denialism, biases, privilege, and the journey forward, on both a personal and a national level. Within each chapter, Phyllis offers insights on these topics, and stories from her personal journey, which co-author and Métis scholar, Kristy McLeod, helps readers further navigate.\n\n\nKihiani: Because We Must\nAuthor: Susan Aglukark\nDescription: An Inuk artist’s journey to healing and self-discovery, tracing her life from childhood in Rankin Inlet and Arviat to her rise as an award-winning musician and her long journey of healing from childhood sexual abuse. The disruption and milestones, the turmoil and joy, the devastation and healing—this is Susan Aglukark’s story of discovering her Inuk self.\n\n\nOur Ancestors’ Kitchen\nAuthor: Willie Poll\nDescription: A poignant and poetic picture book celebrating traditional Indigenous harvesting, foods, and the connections that these build for all our relations.\n\n\n21 Things You Need to Know About Indigenous Self-Government\nAuthor: Bob Joseph\nDescription: With his trademark wisdom, humility, and deep understanding, Bob Joseph shows us the path forward in 21 Things™ You Need to Know About Indigenous Self-Government: A Conversation About Dismantling the Indian Act, in which Indigenous self-governance is already happening and not to be feared—and negotiating more such arrangements, sooner rather than later, is an absolute necessity.\n\n\nI Am on Indigenous Land\nAuthor: Katrina M. Phillips\nDescription: With simple, lyrical text and vibrant panoramic illustrations, this picture book explores the beauty and resources of thirteen ancestral Indigenous lands and how we all use those lands each day. Simple poetry and portrayals of children and families enjoying rivers, bike paths, beaches, and parks show how everyday activities can include honouring Indigenous nations and cultures.\n\nThis list is just a starting point for continued learning and reflection. We encourage everyone to read, reflect, and take meaningful action this Truth and Reconciliation Week.\nPlease visit our resources webpage for more recommended books for all ages, audiobooks, podcasts, and more.\n"
      }
    ]
  },
