      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g6161660",
    "@type": [
      "https://schema.org/BlogPosting"
    ],
    "https://schema.org/text": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "World Health Day: Honouring Equity Through Jordan’s Principle\n"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g6161700",
    "@type": [
      "https://schema.org/BlogPosting"
    ],
    "https://schema.org/text": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Each year on April 7, World Health Day raises awareness of global health issues and promotes access to quality healthcare for all. In Canada, this includes recognizing that equitable access to healthcare has not been a reality for Indigenous children, particularly First Nations children living on reserve.\nJordan’s Principle exists because of this inequity.\nNamed in memory of Jordan River Anderson, a young boy from Norway House Cree Nation, Jordan’s story reflects the consequences of gaps in care. He was born with complex medical needs and spent his life in a hospital while the provincial and federal governments disputed who should pay for his at-home care. He passed away at the age of five without ever living in a family home.\nJordan’s Principle was created to ensure that First Nations children can access the health, social, and educational services they need without delay. It is a child-first policy that places the needs of the child at the centre and ensures that jurisdictional disputes do not prevent access to care.\nSince its full implementation in 2016, Jordan’s Principle has approved over 10.5 million dollars in products, services and supports. These include mental health support, medical equipment, educational assistance, and culturally grounded services. The principle has helped reduce barriers, but it also reflects the broader inequities that continue to impact Indigenous children and families.\nWhile Jordan’s Principle has helped remove barriers, it exists because inequities remain deeply rooted in Canada’s systems.\nIndigenous children continue to face disproportionate health and social challenges shaped by colonial policies and systemic underfunding. Indigenous Peoples in Canada experience lower life expectancy and higher infant mortality rates compared to non-Indigenous populations. For instance, CTV states that as of 2025, in Alberta, the life span of First Nations people is nearly 2 decades shorter than that of other Albertans.\nBroader inequities include higher rates of chronic illness, mental health challenges, and barriers to accessing timely care. These outcomes are the result of systems that have historically failed to provide equitable care and support for Indigenous children and families.\nFor many Indigenous Peoples, health is understood as a balance of physical, emotional, mental, and spiritual well-being. Wellness is connected to land, culture, language, and community. Supporting equitable healthcare must also include supporting Indigenous approaches to wellness and ensuring that services are culturally safe and community-led.\nJordan’s Principle exists to interrupt this pattern by ensuring that First Nations children can access the services they need without delay, disruption, or denial.\nRecent Updates\nRecent developments reflect both continued investment in Jordan’s Principle and ongoing challenges in its implementation.\nIn February 2026, Indigenous Services Minister Mandy Gull-Masty announced a $1.55 billion federal investment to renew Jordan’s Principle funding through March 31, 2027. This funding is intended to help maintain access to essential supports for First Nations children and families and ensure services continue without interruption.\nProgress is also being made through the First Nations Child and Family Services and Jordan’s Principle class action settlement. As of March 2026, more than 136,000 claims have been submitted, and over $650 million has been distributed to First Nations children and families affected by discrimination in health services. This process represents an important step toward accountability and recognition, while also highlighting that this work is ongoing.\nFirst Nations leaders have emphasized that while increased funding is a positive step, it does not resolve the deeper systemic issues affecting the program. Significant backlogs continue to delay access to services, with some requests taking years to be addressed. There are also ongoing concerns about changes to eligibility and how requests are assessed, which can impact whether children receive the supports they need.\nLeaders continue to call for stronger collaboration with First Nations to ensure the program is more responsive to community realities. The volume of requests, particularly for mental health supports, medical services, and educational assistance, reflects the ongoing and urgent needs of First Nations children.\nTogether, these updates reflect both meaningful progress and the continued need to ensure Jordan’s Principle is implemented in a way that is consistent, equitable, and responsive to the needs of First Nations children and families.\nCall to Action\nOn World Health Day, advancing health equity requires collective responsibility and ongoing action.\nLearn about Jordan River Anderson’s story and how Jordan’s Principle came to be. Then share it with others.\nAdvocate for equitable systems that support the delivery of timely, culturally safe, and community-led healthcare for Indigenous children and families.\nExplore the rich, innovative, and time-honoured Indigenous approaches to health and wellness, and help uplift the organizations that deliver these vital services.\nCommitting to reconciliation in action includes ensuring that Indigenous children are not denied the care they need. Supporting Jordan’s Principle is one step toward that commitment.\nSources:\nhttps://sac-isc.gc.ca/eng/1583703111205/1583703134432\nhttps://www150.statcan.gc.ca/n1/daily-quotidien/250603/dq250603b-eng.htm\nhttps://vanierinstitute.ca/families-count-2024/half-of-children-in-foster-care-are-indigenous/\nhttps://www150.statcan.gc.ca/n1/daily-quotidien/241104/dq241104a-eng.htm\nhttps://fncaringsociety.com/jordans-principle\nhttps://ca.news.yahoo.com/jordans-principle-funding-helps-us-025650351.html\nhttps://paherald.sk.ca/gull-masty-makes-progress-new-funding-announced-for-jordans-principle-as-backlog-evaporates/\nhttps://easterndoor.com/article/support-and-hurdles-for-jordans-principle\nhttps://afn.ca/all-news/press-releases/more-than-654-million-paid-through-first-nations-child-and-family-services-and-jordans-principle-class-action-settlement/\n"
      }
    ]
  },
