      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g6315280",
    "@type": [
      "https://schema.org/BlogPosting"
    ],
    "https://schema.org/text": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Millennial Scoop, Bill C‑92, and Indigenous Child Welfare\n"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g6315320",
    "@type": [
      "https://schema.org/BlogPosting"
    ],
    "https://schema.org/text": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The Millennial Scoop refers to the ongoing crisis of Indigenous children being disproportionately removed from their families and communities by child welfare systems. This is a continuation of harms seen in the Sixties Scoop era, where Indigenous children were taken and placed in non‑Indigenous care, with devastating effects on identity, culture, and family ties.\nWhile Indigenous children make up less than 8% of children under 15 in Canada, they account for over 50% of children in foster care (Statistics Canada). Indigenous children are 14 times more likely to be placed in foster care than non-Indigenous children, and many experience prolonged stays in homes far from their families and communities.\nThis systemic pattern is a continuation of colonial practices that prioritize removal over culturally grounded prevention and family support. Indigenous families often face disproportionate scrutiny by child welfare services, which frequently make decisions without adequate recognition of Indigenous culture or community context.\nBill C-92\nIn 2019, the Government of Canada introduced Bill C-92, An Act Respecting First Nations, Inuit, and Métis Children, Youth, and Families (the Act). Co-developed with Indigenous, provincial, and territorial partners, the legislation returns authority over child welfare to Indigenous nations and communities, enabling the development of child and family services grounded in Indigenous laws, traditions, and cultural values. At its core, the Act seeks to ensure that Indigenous children and youth remain connected to their families, communities, and culture.\nBill C-92 marked the first time the federal government exercised its jurisdiction to legislate in Indigenous child welfare, responding directly to the Truth and Reconciliation Commission’s Call to Action (CTA) #4.\nCTA #4: We call upon the federal government to enact Aboriginal child-welfare legislation that establishes national standards for Aboriginal child apprehension and custody cases and includes principles that:\n\nAffirm the right of Aboriginal governments to establish and maintain their own child-welfare agencies.\nRequire all child-welfare agencies and courts to take the residential school legacy into account in their decision-making.\nEstablish, as an important priority, a requirement that placements of Aboriginal children into temporary and permanent care be culturally appropriate.\n\nImplementation Issues\nDespite its promises, Bill C-92 faces many issues in practice. A study done by the Yellowhead Institute highlights key challenges, including:\n\nNational standards: Unclear frameworks for consistent service delivery\nJurisdiction: Tensions between federal, provincial/territorial, and Indigenous governance\nFunding: Uncertainty about long-term, sustainable financial support\nAccountability: Limited mechanisms to ensure transparency and equity\nData collection: Scarce and inconsistent data on outcomes for Indigenous children and families\nResearch gaps: Need for community-driven studies to evaluate effectiveness\n\nLooking Ahead\nBill C‑92 represents real progress in addressing systemic discrimination in child welfare, but its effectiveness depends on full, consistent implementation. Ensuring success requires:\n\nSustained collaboration between Indigenous communities, governments, and child welfare agencies\nClear standards and enforcement mechanisms for culturally safe services\nReliable funding to support Indigenous-led systems\nAccountability and transparent reporting on outcomes for children and families\n\nIndigenous children should grow up connected to family, community, and culture. Bill C‑92 provides a legal foundation to achieve that goal, but there is more work to be done to make that a reality.\nWhat Can We Do?\n\nEducate yourself and others about the historic and ongoing system of Indigenous child removals\nAdvocate for the full enforcement of Bill C-92\nSupport Indigenous-led child and family services\nListen to and amplify Indigenous voices\nShow up in solidarity in person, online, or in any way you can\n\nSources:\n\nhttps://www150.statcan.gc.ca/n1/pub/41-20-0002/412000022024001-eng.htm\nhttps://www.canada.ca/en/indigenous-services-canada/news/2019/06/an-act-respecting-first-nations-inuit-and-metis-children-youth-and-families-receives-royal-assent.html\nhttps://yellowheadinstitute.org/resources/the-promise-and-pitfalls-of-c-92-an-act-respecting-first-nations-metis-and-inuit-children-youth-and-families/\nhttps://www.uottawa.ca/faculty-social-sciences/news-all/developments-challenges-implementing-bill-92-canada-critical-rapid-review\n\n"
      }
    ]
  },
