    ],
    "https://schema.org/headline": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "AI Policy at DWF        "
      }
    ],
    "https://schema.org/text": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The Gord Downie & Chanie Wenjack Fund uses secure, purpose-built AI technologies to improve how we deliver resources and support our network. These tools help keep information up to date using publicly available data, reducing delays, minimizing waste, and ensuring materials reach their intended destination. Privacy is important; no personal information is shared outside of DWF, and you can opt out at any time.\n            "
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://downiewenjack.ca/temporary/af722485-4c89-4150-b334-51f181f10c21",
    "@type": [
      "https://schema.org/CreativeWork"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasDerivedFrom": [
      {
        "@id": "https://downiewenjack.ca/holidaygiftguide-2025/"
      }
    ],
    "https://schema.org/text": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "About DWF\nInspired by Chanie’s story and Gord’s call to build a better Canada, the Gord Downie & Chanie Wenjack Fund aims to build cultural understanding and create a path toward reconciliation between Indigenous and non-Indigenous peoples. Our goal is to improve the lives of Indigenous people by building awareness, education, and connections between all peoples in Canada.\nSubscribe to our Newsletter\n"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://downiewenjack.ca/temporary/467aad57-1581-4258-90e6-62d81fb76f35",
    "@type": [
      "https://schema.org/CreativeWork"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasDerivedFrom": [
      {
        "@id": "https://downiewenjack.ca/holidaygiftguide-2025/"
      }
    ],
    "https://schema.org/text": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Get In Touch\nE-mail: staff@downiewenjack.ca\nToll-free: 1-833-666-2414\nLocal: 519-964-0393\nAddress:\nPO BOX 749\nOHSWEKEN, ON\nN0A1M0\n"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g6522860",
    "@type": [
      "https://schema.org/ImageObject"
    ],
    "https://schema.org/thumbnailUrl": [
      {
        "@id": "https://downiewenjack.ca/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/215blogchrista-1.jpg"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g6522880",
    "@type": [
      "https://schema.org/BlogPosting"
    ],
    "https://schema.org/text": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Beyond 215: A Conversation with Christa Big Canoe\n"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g6522920",
    "@type": [
      "https://schema.org/BlogPosting"
    ],
    "https://schema.org/text": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Content warning: This post contains information about residential schools. Mental health support is available.\nThis year marks ten years since the Truth and Reconciliation Commission (TRC) released its Final Report and 94 Calls to Action. It is also four years since the recovery of 215 unmarked graves at the former Kamloops Residential School, a moment that forced all people in Canada to confront truths Indigenous people have carried for generations.\nTo reflect on this moment, DWF spoke with Christa Big Canoe about her interview featured on A DAY TO LISTEN 2025. Christa is an Anishinaabekwe from Georgina Island First Nation. She is a lawyer, mother, and Director at Aboriginal Legal Services, where she has worked since 2011. She has appeared at every level of court, including the Supreme Court of Canada, and was Senior and then Lead Commission Counsel for the National Inquiry into Missing and Murdered Indigenous Women and Girls (MMIWG).\nSurvivors, Trauma, and the Weight of Ten Years\nFor Christa, the TRC was and remains about Survivors. In her work, she witnesses the ongoing impacts of intergenerational trauma caused by colonial policies. She sees poverty, incarceration, and systemic inequities that remain unchanged, even a decade after the TRC’s Final Report.\nShe also cautions that history can repeat itself. “If we don’t name and recognize what has happened, it can happen again,” she shares. Today, Indigenous families continue to be torn apart by systems of child welfare and incarceration.\nThe Promise of 94 Calls to Action\nWhen the TRC released the 94 Calls to Action in 2015, Christa was struck that there were only 94. A decade later, less than one quarter of the Calls have been completed (Indigenous Watchdog, 2025).\nIn her interview for A DAY TO LISTEN, Christa pointed to Call to Action 41, which led to the MMIWG National Inquiry. While the government may claim it has fulfilled this call, it has failed to implement the Inquiry’s final recommendations. She also noted that the TRC should have explicitly named genocide, rather than limiting its findings to “cultural genocide.”\nAt the same time, Christa acknowledges that the TRC changed the conversation in Canada. “There’s a generation that is coming up that has learned more than any other,” she says. Young people today are more informed about residential schools than their parents or grandparents ever were. This cultural shift is important, but the gap between symbolic commitments and meaningful action remains wide.\n215+ \nChrista also spoke about the recovery of 215 unmarked graves in Kamloops. While it brought urgent attention to the issue of missing children, she worries it has risked being reduced to a slogan.\n“The number of gravesites keeps growing,” she says. She stresses that the way these children died denied families the right to grieve properly and stripped communities of their ceremonial rights. “It took away culture, not just from the deceased but from those who lost someone.”\nFormer TRC chair Justice Murray Sinclair estimated the number of missing children could be between 10,000 and 20,000. Christa notes that when Kamloops was reported in 2021, public reports outlining these truths were already available. “We never needed 215 to know what happened to our people,” she reminds us.  \nDenialism and Responsibility\nDespite overwhelming evidence, residential school denialism persists. “We still have people denying missing children actually happened, even though it’s well documented,” Christa stated in the interview.\nShe goes on to explain that denialism is always harmful and that it retraumatizes Survivors by making them feel unbelieved. It dismisses the oral histories and documented truths that communities have carried for decades, often devolving into a harmful “prove it to me” mentality.\nYouth and Education\nChrista sees hope in young people. She believes that parents today are more engaged and shares, “When you empower kids with knowledge at a young age, it automatically starts dialogue. Age-appropriate curriculum is crucial for this.” She recommends resources that build knowledge, including Tamara Starblanket’s book Suffer the Little Children: Genocide, Indigenous Nations and the Canadian State, which argues Canada’s residential school system was an act of genocide.\nMoving from Awareness to Action\nChrista believes there are many ways for everyone in Canada to engage: \nLearn: Listen to podcasts and audiobooks, read books, and seek out Indigenous-created media. Combat denialism with knowledge. \nTake the Calls to Action Seriously: Spend 94 days reading one Call to Action each day. Reflect on how each one applies to your life, workplace, or community. \nSupport Indigenous-led Initiatives: Back community searches, ceremonies, and healing projects. Support Indigenous economies beyond symbolic times of the year. \nLook beyond the Orange: Christa urges us to move past symbolic gestures and ensure our commitments are sustained year-round. \nConfront Denialism: When misinformation appears, speak up. Truth matters. \nOnce You Know, You Cannot Unknow\nTen years after the TRC’s Final Report and 94 Calls to Action, more young people understand the truth of residential schools than ever before. But awareness without action does little to change the realities Indigenous communities face each day.\nChrista’s words are clear. “Once you know something, you can’t unknow it.” The question is: what are you going to do about it?\nThe children who never made it home from residential school deserve more than to be remembered as a number. They, their families, and the Survivors who shared their truth deserve justice, action, and a commitment that moves beyond awareness into real change.\nLearn more by listening to Christa’s interview on A DAY TO LISTEN by tuning in to a participating radio station on September 30 or on our website at adaytolisten.ca. \n"
      }
    ]
  },
