    ],
    "https://schema.org/text": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Get In Touch\nE-mail: staff@downiewenjack.ca\nToll-free: 1-833-666-2414\nLocal: 519-964-0393\nAddress:\nPO BOX 749\nOHSWEKEN, ON\nN0A1M0\n"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g6573560",
    "@type": [
      "https://schema.org/ImageObject"
    ],
    "https://schema.org/thumbnailUrl": [
      {
        "@id": "https://downiewenjack.ca/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/IndigenousFood.jpg"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g6573580",
    "@type": [
      "https://schema.org/BlogPosting"
    ],
    "https://schema.org/text": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Indigenous Food: Rooted in Land, Culture, and Resilience\n"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g6573620",
    "@type": [
      "https://schema.org/BlogPosting"
    ],
    "https://schema.org/text": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Indigenous food traditions throughout Turtle Island reflect deep relationships with the land, water, and seasonal cycles. These foodways carry knowledge, ceremony, and identity.\nFor many Inuit communities, traditional foods are referred to as “country food,” and are harvested through time-honoured practices that reflect respect for the land and animals. This includes Arctic char, seal, whale, and caribou. They provide essential nutrients and remain vital to health and well-being in northern communities, especially where access to store-bought food is limited or costly.\nIn First Nations and Métis communities, dishes such as wild rice, salmon, moose, and elk remain staples, often cooked over open fires or shared during seasonal gatherings. Bannock, a type of fried bread, is also commonly served. Today, bannock is often prepared with care at community events, powwows, and feasts, and has become a symbol of Indigenous adaptation and survival.\nRubaboo is a stew or porridge that is unique to the Métis. The Plains Cree often hunted bison for food, as well as clothing and materials for shelter. The well-known Indigenous dish, Three Sisters, consists of corn, beans, and squash and comes from the Haudenosaunee. These traditional food practices are rooted in knowledge passed down through generations.\nUltimately, Indigenous food is inseparable from land. Harvesting, fishing, hunting, and gathering are acts of connection and responsibility. Indigenous food is about decolonizing and reclaiming access to traditional knowledge and food systems. Reclaiming Indigenous food is about reclaiming culture and self-determination.\n\nSources:\nFood and Hunting | Haudenosaunee Confederacy\nInuit Country Food in Canada | The Canadian Encyclopedia\nTraditional Metis Foods and Food Preparation | Metis Museum\n"
      }
    ]
  },
