        "@value": "AI Policy at DWF        "
      }
    ],
    "https://schema.org/text": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The Gord Downie & Chanie Wenjack Fund uses secure, purpose-built AI technologies to improve how we deliver resources and support our network. These tools help keep information up to date using publicly available data, reducing delays, minimizing waste, and ensuring materials reach their intended destination. Privacy is important; no personal information is shared outside of DWF, and you can opt out at any time.\n            "
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://downiewenjack.ca/temporary/212c2197-cf3f-45bd-bdca-6db7e81dcce5",
    "@type": [
      "https://schema.org/CreativeWork"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasDerivedFrom": [
      {
        "@id": "https://downiewenjack.ca/indigenous-food-rooted-in-land-culture-and-resilience/"
      }
    ],
    "https://schema.org/text": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "About DWF\nInspired by Chanie’s story and Gord’s call to build a better Canada, the Gord Downie & Chanie Wenjack Fund aims to build cultural understanding and create a path toward reconciliation between Indigenous and non-Indigenous peoples. Our goal is to improve the lives of Indigenous people by building awareness, education, and connections between all peoples in Canada.\nSubscribe to our Newsletter\n"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://downiewenjack.ca/temporary/ac22058c-3fe1-4f5d-a528-a6fc6180e56d",
    "@type": [
      "https://schema.org/CreativeWork"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasDerivedFrom": [
      {
        "@id": "https://downiewenjack.ca/indigenous-food-rooted-in-land-culture-and-resilience/"
      }
    ],
    "https://schema.org/text": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Get In Touch\nE-mail: staff@downiewenjack.ca\nToll-free: 1-833-666-2414\nLocal: 519-964-0393\nAddress:\nPO BOX 749\nOHSWEKEN, ON\nN0A1M0\n"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g6625260",
    "@type": [
      "https://schema.org/BlogPosting"
    ],
    "https://schema.org/text": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "10th Anniversary of the TRC Final Reports\n"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g6625300",
    "@type": [
      "https://schema.org/BlogPosting"
    ],
    "https://schema.org/text": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "It’s been 10 years since the Truth and Reconciliation Commission (TRC) of Canada released its Final Reports and 94 Calls to Action. This June, we reflect on the impacts of residential schools, how far we’ve come, and how far we still need to go.\n\nWhat was the TRC?\nThe Truth and Reconciliation Commission was established in 2008 as part of the Residential Schools Settlement Agreement. Its purpose was to:\n\nDocument the history and lasting impacts of residential schools\nProvide a safe space for Survivors to share their stories\nPromote public education and awareness\nLay the foundation for reconciliation between Indigenous and non-Indigenous peoples\n\nThe TRC spent 6 years travelling to all parts of Canada and heard from more than 6,500 witnesses, many of whom were residential school Survivors. In 2015, the TRC released its Final Reports, along with 94 Calls to Action to guide Canada’s path toward reconciliation.\n\nWhat are the 94 Calls to Action?\nThe 94 Calls to Action are recommendations made by the TRC for governments, institutions, and individuals to address the negative impacts of residential schools and advance meaningful reconciliation.\nThey cover areas like:\n\nChild Welfare\nEducation\nLanguage & Culture\nJustice\nHealth\n\n\nWhat Calls to Action have Been Completed?\n10 years later, some progress has been made; however, only 15 of the 94 Calls to Action have been fulfilled.\nSome of the completed calls include:\n13. Acknowledge that Indigenous rights include Indigenous language rights\n15. Appoint an Indigenous Languages Commissioner\n16. Create university and college degree and diploma programs in Indigenous languages\n39. Collect and publish data on the criminal victimization of Indigenous people\n41. Appoint a public inquiry into the cause of, and remedies for, the disproportionate victimization of Indigenous women and girls\n59. Church parties to the Settlement Agreement to educate congregations on why apologies are necessary\n70. A national review of archival policies\n80. Establish a National Day for Truth and Reconciliation as a statutory holiday\n83. Canada Council for the Arts to establish a strategy for Indigenous and non-Indigenous artists to undertake collaborative projects\n84. Restore and increase funding to the CBC/Radio-Canada to enable it to support reconciliation\n85. Aboriginal Peoples Television Network to support reconciliation\n88. Continued support for the North American Indigenous Games\n94. Replace the Oath of Citizenship\nThere is more work to be done.\n\nKey Developments Since 2015\nWhile the 94 Calls to Action remain a key framework for reconciliation, other important steps have also been taken over the past 10 years:\n\nIndigenous Child Welfare Reform (2024) – Canada is working to address Indigenous child welfare, including a settlement for discriminatory underfunding of First Nations child and family services.\nPapal Apology (2022) – Pope Francis travelled to Canada and issued an apology for the Catholic Church’s role in residential schools.\nImplementation of UNDRIP (2021) – Canada passed legislation in 2021 to implement the United Nations Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples (UNDRIP).\nMMIWG2S+ Inquiry (2019) – National Inquiry into Missing and Murdered Indigenous Women and Girls released their Final Report.\nIndigenous Languages Act (2019) – The Indigenous Languages Act was introduced to help revitalize Indigenous languages. This piece of legislation aims to support and preserve Indigenous Languages.\nRepresentation in Government and Media – As of 2025, there has been an increase in Indigenous representation in government and media, providing opportunities to amplify Indigenous voices.\n\nThese milestones show that reconciliation is happening on many fronts—but the Calls to Action remain an essential guide for ongoing progress.\n\nHow you can Help\nReconciliation is a shared responsibility, and there’s more work to be done. Here are some ideas on how you can help:\n\nEducate Yourself: Read, watch, and listen to Indigenous-led work.\nChallenge Stereotypes: Speak up against racism and misinformation.\nSupport Indigenous Initiatives: Walk for Wenjack, add your event to our calendar, or bring reconciliation to your school or workplace.\nCorporate Commitment: Companies can build respectful partnerships with Indigenous communities by committing to Free, Prior, and Informed Consent and sharing the long-term benefits of economic projects. Ensure Indigenous peoples have fair access to education, employment, and leadership opportunities.\n\nThe TRC laid out a path toward reconciliation. It’s up to all of us to keep walking it. Let’s build the next 10 years together.\nSubmit your hopes for the next 10 years and beyond:\n"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
