        "@value": "Privacy Policy Community Guidelines\n"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://downiewenjack.ca/temporary/e432c558-e11c-4749-81e7-ad589238a34b",
    "@type": [
      "https://schema.org/CreativeWork"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasDerivedFrom": [
      {
        "@id": "https://downiewenjack.ca/10th-anniversary-of-the-trc-final-reports/"
      }
    ],
    "https://schema.org/headline": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "AI Policy at DWF        "
      }
    ],
    "https://schema.org/text": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The Gord Downie & Chanie Wenjack Fund uses secure, purpose-built AI technologies to improve how we deliver resources and support our network. These tools help keep information up to date using publicly available data, reducing delays, minimizing waste, and ensuring materials reach their intended destination. Privacy is important; no personal information is shared outside of DWF, and you can opt out at any time.\n            "
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://downiewenjack.ca/temporary/940f245b-2484-493b-9a47-b640fde2ffe7",
    "@type": [
      "https://schema.org/CreativeWork"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasDerivedFrom": [
      {
        "@id": "https://downiewenjack.ca/10th-anniversary-of-the-trc-final-reports/"
      }
    ],
    "https://schema.org/text": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "About DWF\nInspired by Chanie’s story and Gord’s call to build a better Canada, the Gord Downie & Chanie Wenjack Fund aims to build cultural understanding and create a path toward reconciliation between Indigenous and non-Indigenous peoples. Our goal is to improve the lives of Indigenous people by building awareness, education, and connections between all peoples in Canada.\nSubscribe to our Newsletter\n"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://downiewenjack.ca/temporary/571f09a7-535c-42b8-ab61-7fec931ba6fd",
    "@type": [
      "https://schema.org/CreativeWork"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasDerivedFrom": [
      {
        "@id": "https://downiewenjack.ca/10th-anniversary-of-the-trc-final-reports/"
      }
    ],
    "https://schema.org/text": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Get In Touch\nE-mail: staff@downiewenjack.ca\nToll-free: 1-833-666-2414\nLocal: 519-964-0393\nAddress:\nPO BOX 749\nOHSWEKEN, ON\nN0A1M0\n"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g6676920",
    "@type": [
      "https://schema.org/BlogPosting"
    ],
    "https://schema.org/text": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Recommended Reading: Two-Spirit Activists and Authors\n"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g6676960",
    "@type": [
      "https://schema.org/BlogPosting"
    ],
    "https://schema.org/text": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "As we deepen our understanding of gender, sexuality, and identity through an Indigenous lens, it’s crucial to amplify the voices of Two-Spirit individuals—those who embody both masculine and feminine spirits, a sacred identity in many Indigenous cultures. Colonization disrupted these roles, leading to erasure and marginalization. This reading list showcases works by Two-Spirit authors and artists exploring identity, resilience, and community through memoir, poetry, fiction, and essays, offering vital perspectives on what it means to be Two-Spirit today.\n\n\nA Two-Spirit Journey: The Autobiography of a Lesbian Ojibwa-Cree Elder\nAuthor: Ma-Nee Chacaby (with Mary Louisa Plummer)\nSuggested Ages: Grade 12 and up\nDescription: This memoir chronicles Ma-Nee Chacaby’s life as an Ojibwa-Cree lesbian, detailing her experiences with abuse, addiction, and her journey toward sobriety and self-acceptance. Her story offers insight into the challenges faced by Indigenous 2SLGBTQIA+ individuals and highlights her activism within these communities.\n\n\nCeremonies for the Dead\nAuthor: Gwen Benaway\nSuggested Ages: Grades 11 and up\nDescription: This poetry collection by Gwen Benaway, a Two-Spirit trans woman of Anishinaabe and Métis descent, addresses themes of gender identity, colonialism, and survival. Her work provides a poignant look into the lived experiences of Two-Spirit individuals navigating complex cultural landscapes.\n\n\nSovereign Erotics: A Collection of Two-Spirit Literature\nEdited by Qwo-Li Driskill, Daniel Heath Justice, Deborah Miranda, and Lisa Tatonetti\nSuggested Ages: Grades 11 and up\nDescription: This anthology brings together fiction, poetry, and essays from Two-Spirit writers across North America. It celebrates the diversity and resilience of Two-Spirit people, offering a rich tapestry of voices that challenge colonial narratives and affirm Indigenous queer identities.\n\n\nIn Her I Am\nAuthor: Chrystos\nSuggested Ages: Grades 11 and up\nDescription: Chrystos, a Two-Spirit lesbian activist and poet, presents a collection of poems that explore survival, desire, and the politics of urban Indigenous life. Her work is a powerful testament to the strength and creativity of Two-Spirit individuals.\n\n\nJonny Appleseed\nAuthor: Joshua Whitehead\nSuggested Ages: Grades 11 and up\nDescription: This debut novel follows a Two-Spirit, Indigiqueer young man as he navigates life in the city and his return to the reservation for his stepfather’s funeral. The narrative explores themes of identity, community, and resilience\n\n\nshe walks for days inside a thousand eyes: a two-spirit story\nAuthor: Sharron Proulx-Turner\nSuggested Ages: Grades 11 and up\nDescription: In she walks for days inside a thousand eyes (a two-spirit story), Sharron Proulx-Turner combines poetry and history to delve into the little-known lives of Two-Spirit women. Regarded with both wonder and fear when first encountered by the West, First Nations women living with masculine and feminine principles in the same body had important roles to play in society, as healers and visionaries, before they were suppressed during the colonial invasion.\n\n\nnîtisânak\nAuthor: Jas M. Morgan\nSuggested Ages: Grades 11 and up\nDescription: A groundbreaking memoir spanning nations, prairie punk scenes, and queer love stories, Jas M. Morgan’s nîtisânak is woven around grief over the loss of their mother. It also explores despair and healing through community and family, and being torn apart by the same. Using cyclical narrative techniques and drawing on Morgan’s Cree, Saulteaux, and Métis ancestral teachings, this work offers a compelling perspective on the connections that must be broken and the ones that heal.\n\n\nLove Beyond Body, Space, and Time\nEditor: Hope Nicholson\nSuggested Ages: Grades 10 and up\nDescription: A collection of Indigenous science fiction and urban fantasy focusing on 2SLGBTQIA+ and Two-Spirit characters. These stories range from a transgender woman trying an experimental transition medication to young lovers separated through decades and meeting far in their own future.\n\n\nMaking Love with the Land\nAuthor: Joshua Whitehead\nSuggested Ages: Grades 10 and up\nDescription: In this collection of essays, Joshua Whitehead, an Oji-Cree Two-Spirit author, delves into the intersections of Indigeneity, queerness, and mental health. Through personal narratives, he explores the complexities of identity and the healing power of storytelling.\n\n\nCosmo, From the Stars\nCo-created by Itai Jeffries (Yésah/Occaneechi) and illustrated by Trae Middlebrooks\nSuggested Ages: Grades 3 to 6\nDescription: This children’s book features a Two-Spirit character and explores themes of identity and belonging. The story is designed to provide representation and support for Two-Spirit children and their families (PDF).\n\n\n47,000 Beads\nAuthor: Koja Adeyoha, Angel Adeyoha Illustrator: Holly McGillis\nSuggested Ages: Grades 1 to 3\nDescription: Peyton loves to dance, especially at Pow Wow, but her Auntie notices that she’s been dancing less and less. When Peyton shares that she isn’t comfortable wearing a dress anymore, Auntie Eyota asks some friends for help to get Peyton what she needs.\n\nThis list is just a starting point for continued learning and reflection. We encourage everyone to listen to, share, and amplify Two-Spirit voices – and take meaningful action toward reconciliation.\n"
      }
    ]
  },
