    ],
    "https://schema.org/headline": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "AI Policy at DWF        "
      }
    ],
    "https://schema.org/text": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The Gord Downie & Chanie Wenjack Fund uses secure, purpose-built AI technologies to improve how we deliver resources and support our network. These tools help keep information up to date using publicly available data, reducing delays, minimizing waste, and ensuring materials reach their intended destination. Privacy is important; no personal information is shared outside of DWF, and you can opt out at any time.\n            "
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://downiewenjack.ca/temporary/676d85ba-6476-4145-95ec-b4d3af79212c",
    "@type": [
      "https://schema.org/CreativeWork"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasDerivedFrom": [
      {
        "@id": "https://downiewenjack.ca/recommended-reading-two-spirit-activists-and-authors/"
      }
    ],
    "https://schema.org/text": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "About DWF\nInspired by Chanie’s story and Gord’s call to build a better Canada, the Gord Downie & Chanie Wenjack Fund aims to build cultural understanding and create a path toward reconciliation between Indigenous and non-Indigenous peoples. Our goal is to improve the lives of Indigenous people by building awareness, education, and connections between all peoples in Canada.\nSubscribe to our Newsletter\n"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://downiewenjack.ca/temporary/604d12dd-c911-4b95-b650-110426b4c95e",
    "@type": [
      "https://schema.org/CreativeWork"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasDerivedFrom": [
      {
        "@id": "https://downiewenjack.ca/recommended-reading-two-spirit-activists-and-authors/"
      }
    ],
    "https://schema.org/text": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Get In Touch\nE-mail: staff@downiewenjack.ca\nToll-free: 1-833-666-2414\nLocal: 519-964-0393\nAddress:\nPO BOX 749\nOHSWEKEN, ON\nN0A1M0\n"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g6727560",
    "@type": [
      "https://schema.org/BlogPosting"
    ],
    "https://schema.org/text": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Facing the Facts: Why Residential School Denialism Must Be Called Out\n"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g6727600",
    "@type": [
      "https://schema.org/BlogPosting"
    ],
    "https://schema.org/text": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Content warning: This post contain information about residential schools. Mental health support is available.\n\nIn February 2025, a troubling story out of British Columbia made headlines and reignited a painful conversation.\nDallas Brodie, a former Member of the Legislative Assembly of the BC Conservative Party, publicly supported lawyer James Heller, who had filed a legal challenge against a mandatory cultural competency course for lawyers in the province. The course includes education about residential schools and the findings at the former Kamloops Residential School site.\nHeller’s objection? He claimed the Law Society of BC was misleading the public by not emphasizing the word “potential” when describing the 215 unmarked graves detected through ground-penetrating radar in Kamloops. Brodie doubled down on this rhetoric, stating that the number of “confirmed child burials” at Kamloops was zero, despite the fact that the Tk’emlúps te Secwépemc First Nation had already confirmed the findings based on both technology and community knowledge.\nThese remarks were quickly and widely condemned.\nAn Act of “Racist Residential School Denialism”\nThe Union of BC Indian Chiefs called Brodie’s comments an act of “racist residential school denialism,” and demanded a public apology to Survivors. Indigenous leaders and organizations emphasized that this kind of language, framing the findings as speculative, ultimately ignores the oral histories and lived experiences of Indigenous people.\nJust days later, Brodie was expelled from the Conservative caucus.  \nWhat is Residential School Denialism and Why is it a Concern?\nResidential school denialism doesn’t always come in the form of outright rejection of history. More often, it shows up as doubt, deflection, and demands for more “proof” – even in the face of well-documented truth.\nIt’s not just offensive. It’s dangerous.\nDenying, minimizing, or reframing the violence and deaths that took place in residential schools retraumatizes Survivors, undermines truth-telling, and halts progress toward reconciliation.\nThe Truth and Reconciliation Commission of Canada (TRC) made clear that more than 150,000 Indigenous children were taken from their families and placed in residential schools, and many never returned home. The TRC documented thousands of deaths and stated that the true number is likely much higher.\nSince 2021, Indigenous communities across the country have been leading investigations at former residential school sites, using ground-penetrating radar to locate unmarked graves. These are not discoveries; they are confirmations of what Survivors and families have been telling us for generations.\nLanguage Matters\nWhen people question whether the graves are “confirmed,” they’re not just scrutinizing a word. They’re casting doubt on the lived truth of Survivors. \nThe word “recovery” is used intentionally by many Indigenous communities. Not because the children were lost, but because they were taken. These are not new truths. They are truths that have finally been acknowledged by wider Canada. Words like “discovery” or “alleged” erase that.\nWhat We Can Do\nIf you’re reading this, you likely care deeply about truth, justice, and reconciliation. So, what can we do when denialism shows up? \n\nCall it out. When you see misinformation, whether in the news, online, or in conversation, correct it. Share verified facts and Indigenous-led sources. \nCentre Survivors. Trust the stories of those who lived through residential schools. Share and uplift their voices.\nSupport Indigenous-led search and memorial efforts. Many communities are fundraising for ground searches, ceremonies, and healing.\nEducate yourself and others. Recommended reading below:  \n\nThe TRC’s Final Report and 94 Calls to Action.  \n“But They Had Good Intentions”: Indian Residential School Denialism  \n\n\n\nTruth Before Reconciliation \nThe 215+ Pledge exists to honour the children who never came home, and to call people into action – not just for a moment, but for the long haul.\nThe events of the past few months are a stark reminder that the truth is still under attack. And that’s why this work remains urgent.\nTake the 215+ Pledge. Stand up to denialism and be a part of truth-telling. \n\nResources:  \nB.C. Opposition law critic defies Rustad’s request to delete ‘zero graves’ comment – The Globe and Mail \nUBCIC Rejects MLA Dallas Brodie’s Purported ‘Truth-Seeking’ as Racist Residential School Denialism; Calls for Apology to Survivors – UBCIC \nB.C. Conservative leader kicks Dallas Brodie out of caucus for ‘mocking’ residential school testimony | CBC News \nResidential School History – NCTR \nResidential school denialism: what is it and how to recognize it | CBC News \nConfronting residential schools denialism is an ethical and shared Canadian responsibility \nUniversity says event with One BC leader on residential schools not permitted – The Turtle Island News \n\nAbout the Author\nQuinn Roffey- Antoine is Plains Cree from Poundmaker Cree Nation in Treaty 6 Territory. She grew up in Toronto, Ontario and resides there now. \n"
      }
    ]
  },
