    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g6779220",
    "@type": [
      "https://schema.org/BlogPosting"
    ],
    "https://schema.org/text": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The Treaty of Waitangi & Treaty Principles Bill\n"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g6779260",
    "@type": [
      "https://schema.org/BlogPosting"
    ],
    "https://schema.org/text": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "What is the Treaty of Waitangi?\nTe Tiriti o Waitangi (known in English as the Treaty of Waitangi) is Aotearoa New Zealand’s founding document, signed in 1840 between representatives of the British Crown and over 500 Māori Rangatiras (chiefs). It aimed to establish a partnership and unity between Māori and the British but contained significant differences in interpretation between the English and te reo Māori versions.\nWhile it promised Māori the rights of British citizens, the two versions differed on the extent of power rangatiras were ceding over their affairs, lands, and autonomy. Over time, the Crown breached both versions, leading to the loss of Māori lands, suppression of language and culture, and significant disadvantages for Māori communities.\nHow was the Treaty revived?\nSince the 1970s, there has been greater public awareness and efforts to honour the treaty and its principles. Prompted by a strong Māori protest movement, New Zealand’s courts, lawmakers, and the Waitangi Tribunal have worked to address breaches of the Treaty of Waitangi.\nEfforts to honour the treaty have led to societal changes, including a Māori language revival, increased treaty recognition in law, and targeted policies and programs to address disparities. Settlements have also been made for land and resource expropriations, though challenges for Māori remain.\nWhat is the Treaty Principles Bill?\nNew Zealand is currently debating a controversial bill introduced by ACT (a minor party in the ruling centre-right coalition) to legally define the principles of the Treaty of Waitangi. While the treaty’s principles are already embedded in laws to address historical wrongs against Māori, ACT argues that current interpretations divide the country by race and seek to shift treaty interpretation from courts to parliament.\nThe bill reinterprets the Treaty of Waitangi by proposing new principles:\n\nPrinciple 1: The Executive Government of New Zealand has full power to govern, and the Parliament of New Zealand has full power to make laws: (a) in the best interests of everyone; and (b) in accordance with the rule of law and the maintenance of a free and democratic society.\nPrinciple 2: (1) The Crown recognises and will respect and protect the rights that hapū and iwi Māori had under the Treaty of Waitangi/te Tiriti o Waitangi at the time they signed it. (2) However, if those rights differ from the rights of everyone, subclause (1) applies only if those rights are agreed in the settlement of a historical treaty claim under the Treaty of Waitangi Act 1975.\nPrinciple 3: (1) Everyone is equal before the law. (2) Everyone is entitled, without discrimination, to (a) the equal protection and equal benefit of the law; and (b) the equal enjoyment of the same fundamental human rights.\n\nWhat has the reaction been to the bill?\nThe bill was introduced in New Zealand’s parliament on November 14. In response, 22-year-old Maori Party MP Hana-Rawhiti Maipi-Clarke ripped the bill in half and led a haka, which is a ‘war challenge’ or ‘war cry’ traditionally performed by men going to war. Today, the haka has been made famous by New Zealand’s rugby team, the All Blacks, but it is commonly performed at weddings and other gatherings. MP Maipi-Clarke was suspended from Parliament for 24 hours for her behaviour, but the video has since gone viral, amassing over 370 million views and mobilizing supporters both in New Zealand and around the world.\nOutside of parliament, Hīkoi mō te Tiriti (March for the Treaty of Waitangi), organized by Toitū te Tiriti, commenced in the country’s far north at Te Rerenga Wairua (Cape Rēinga) on November 11. This nine-day, 1,000 km hikoi, a peaceful march rooted in Māori tradition, concluded in Wellington on November 19. Over 40,000 protesters gathered at New Zealand’s Parliament in one of the country’s largest-ever protests to oppose a bill.\nWhy Is It Controversial?\nCritics argue these principles may diminish specific Māori rights by emphasizing universal equality over treaty-specific protections. Key concerns include:\n\nRedefining the Treaty: The bill unilaterally reinterprets the Treaty, effectively rewriting a historical agreement and distorting its intent.\nUndermining Māori Rights: It threatens Māori tino rangatiratanga (self-determination) and established principles like partnership, protection, equity, and redress, potentially erasing Māori autonomy.\nConstitutional Concerns: As the Treaty is foundational to New Zealand’s legal and political framework, unilateral changes could undermine trust and the spirit of reconciliation.\nImpact on Reconciliation: The bill risks reversing decades of progress in truth and reconciliation between Māori and the Crown.\nSimplistic Approach: Critics argue it oversimplifies complex issues, disregards nuanced interpretations, and fosters division.\nFlawed Design: The Waitangi Tribunal and legal experts have criticized the bill as flawed, misleading, and inconsistent with the Treaty, emphasizing the need for equity to address entrenched disparities.\n\nWhat’s next for the bill?\nAlthough it passed its first reading in Parliament, it faces significant opposition and is unlikely to progress further. The debate has, however, reignited broader discussions about the Treaty’s place in modern New Zealand, equality versus equity, and how to reconcile historical injustices.\nLearn More\nLearn more through our resources on New Zealand:\n\nhttps://downiewenjack.ca/our-work/legacy-schools-programs/resources/#newzealand\n\nFollow the movement on social media:\nhttps://www.instagram.com/actionstationnz/\nhttps://www.instagram.com/toitu_te_tiriti/\nhttps://www.instagram.com/maori_party/\nhttps://www.instagram.com/tewharewhakaaro\nhttps://www.instagram.com/terunangaotoa\nSources:\nhttps://teara.govt.nz/en/te-tiriti-o-waitangi-the-treaty-of-waitangi\nhttps://www.waitangitribunal.govt.nz/en/about/the-treaty/about-the-treaty\nhttps://time.com/7177153/new-zealand-founding-treaty-maori-protests/\nhttps://www.stuff.co.nz/politics/360487289/explained-treaty-principles-bill\nhttps://www.thepost.co.nz/nz-news/360489023/masses-shoulder-shoulder-opposing-anti-maori-bill\nhttps://our.actionstation.org.nz/petitions/kati-stop-the-introduction-of-the-treaty-principles-bill\nhttps://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/hikoi-to-parliament-live-updates-thousands-descend-on-wellington-in-historic-day-of-mass-protest/HGI2Z5XVYBCXVHNDGPSFGWPFBQ/\nhttps://www.teaonews.co.nz/2024/11/19/i-ripped-that-in-half-and-chucked-it-away-hana-rawhiti-delivers-speech-after-world-shaking-haka/\n"
      }
    ]
  },
