    ],
    "https://schema.org/headline": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "AI Policy at DWF        "
      }
    ],
    "https://schema.org/text": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "The Gord Downie & Chanie Wenjack Fund uses secure, purpose-built AI technologies to improve how we deliver resources and support our network. These tools help keep information up to date using publicly available data, reducing delays, minimizing waste, and ensuring materials reach their intended destination. Privacy is important; no personal information is shared outside of DWF, and you can opt out at any time.\n            "
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://downiewenjack.ca/temporary/d1b0eaeb-6900-4848-bcde-d213643f73e8",
    "@type": [
      "https://schema.org/CreativeWork"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasDerivedFrom": [
      {
        "@id": "https://downiewenjack.ca/indigenous-child-removals-the-sixties-scoop/"
      }
    ],
    "https://schema.org/text": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "About DWF\nInspired by Chanie’s story and Gord’s call to build a better Canada, the Gord Downie & Chanie Wenjack Fund aims to build cultural understanding and create a path toward reconciliation between Indigenous and non-Indigenous peoples. Our goal is to improve the lives of Indigenous people by building awareness, education, and connections between all peoples in Canada.\nSubscribe to our Newsletter\n"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://downiewenjack.ca/temporary/7cd2892f-6739-43ad-85c0-8141611666f1",
    "@type": [
      "https://schema.org/CreativeWork"
    ],
    "http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasDerivedFrom": [
      {
        "@id": "https://downiewenjack.ca/indigenous-child-removals-the-sixties-scoop/"
      }
    ],
    "https://schema.org/text": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Get In Touch\nE-mail: staff@downiewenjack.ca\nToll-free: 1-833-666-2414\nLocal: 519-964-0393\nAddress:\nPO BOX 749\nOHSWEKEN, ON\nN0A1M0\n"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g6948120",
    "@type": [
      "https://schema.org/BlogPosting"
    ],
    "https://schema.org/text": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Educator Profile: Grace Gadon, K-3 Music Specialist, Elizabeth Rummel School, AB\n"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g6948160",
    "@type": [
      "https://schema.org/ImageObject"
    ],
    "https://schema.org/thumbnailUrl": [
      {
        "@id": "https://downiewenjack.ca/wp-content/uploads/2024/01/Student-group-photo.png"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g6948180",
    "@type": [
      "https://schema.org/BlogPosting"
    ],
    "https://schema.org/text": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Grace Gadon’s class taking on the Secret Path through music. Grace’s full lesson plan will be shared in future newsletters.\n‘As an elementary music teacher who is constantly looking for impactful ways to weave Indigenous learning, particularly about residential schools to my students, I came across the song “Run” by Indigenous folk-gospel Juno-award winner, William Prince. Initially chosen for a musical performance for our Terry Fox assembly, when I looked more closely at the lyrics and ‘felt’ the rawness of the song, I thought of Secret Path, which I had been reading again as we neared Truth and Reconciliation Week.\nThis connection between story and song unfolded into a powerful collaborative musical study and performance with one of my Grade 3 music classes. The deeper we looked at Chanie Wenjack’s journey home in Secret Path, the more powerful Prince’s lyrics became for us. “One foot in front of the other for it could mean tomorrow for someone.”\nOur study of connecting music with the understanding of residential schools, represented by stories like Chanie’s, led to an opportunity to perform for and learn from Îyârhe Nakoda Knowledge Keeper, Tracy Stevens and her family’s experiences in residential schools and day schools. We used the Stoney Nakoda Dictionary to translate some of the lyrics in “Run” to the Stoney language while also working at translating others to French as we are a French immersion class.\nAlthough our “journey” learning from Chanie through music is far from over, this study provided a powerful, rich opportunity for both myself and my students to contribute to reconciliACTION this year and I know it will serve as a tool for future reconciliACTIONS with my music students for years to come.’\nGrace Gadon (She/Her)\nB. Mus, B. Ed\nK-3 Music Specialist\nElizabeth Rummel School\n“We are the music makers, and we are the dreamers of dreams.”\n"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "_:g6948240",
    "@type": [
      "https://schema.org/BlogPosting"
    ],
    "https://schema.org/text": [
      {
        "@language": "en",
        "@value": "Send us your ReconciliACTIONS\nWe love seeing and sharing what Legacy Schools are doing! Send us your reconciliACTIONs for a chance to be featured in our ReconciliACTION guidebook, Legacy School materials, and other DWF communications!\n\n\n\nSUBMIT YOUR RECONCILIACTIONS\n\n\n\nShare your ReconciliACTIONS on social media\nWe love seeing the change you’re making – and so does the DWF community! Share your photos and tag us on social media @downiewenjack.\n"
      }
    ]
  },
